How Courts Treat “Conception” and “Reduction to Practice”
U.S. patent law recognizes two foundational pillars of invention proof:

Conception — forming a definite and permanent idea of the invention

Reduction to practice — either actually building/testing it or filing a sufficiently detailed application

This is not theoretical — it’s explicitly recognized in case law and legal commentary.

Conception
Courts define conception as:

“the formation in the mind of the inventor, of a definite and permanent idea of the complete and operative invention.”

Recent Federal Circuit decisions continue to refine this standard, emphasizing that conception does not require certainty the invention will work, nor successful experiments, as long as the idea is definite and complete.

Reduction to Practice
Reduction to practice can be:

Actual — building it and showing it works

Constructive — filing a patent application with enough detail to enable the invention

Both are recognized as valid proof of invention.

